{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by jesse eisenberg. the film is based on the book \"the accidental billionaire\" by ben mezrich and tells the story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the subsequent lawsuits that followed.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and ceo of facebook, alongside a talented ensemble cast that includes andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, justin timberlake as sean parker, and joshua jackson as tyler and zuckerberg's roommate.\n\nthe film's narrative is presented in a non-linear fashion, jumping back and forth in time to depict the early days of facebook and the subsequent fallout between zuckerberg and his friends. the film's script is witty and engaging, with eisenberg delivering a standout performance as the complex and often difficult zuckerberg.\n\none of the film's greatest strengths is its ability to balance humor and drama, never shying away from the more serious aspects of the story. the film's portrayal of thefacebook's early days is often hilarious, with a cast of eccentric and lovable characters that add to the film's charm.\n\nhowever, the film is not without its criticisms. some have argued that the film's portrayal of zuckerberg is overly negative, focusing on his more unsavory qualities rather than his accomplishments. others have criticized the film's depiction of thefacebook's early days, arguing that it glosses over the site's more problematic aspects.\n\ndespite these criticisms, the social network is a film that is well worth watching. fincher's direction is masterful, using a range of cinematic techniques to bring the story to life. the film's score, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is equally impressive, adding a sense of depth and complexity to the film's narrative.\n\nover